WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.808
Cryptographic keys are secret values that require strong randomness.

2
00:00:03.808 --> 00:00:09.901
A key should not be derived from timestamps, usernames, predictable
strings, or ordinary test random functions.

3
00:00:09.901 --> 00:00:13.138
If a key is weak or exposed, the algorithm name may not matter.

4
00:00:13.138 --> 00:00:22.976
Secure key generation should normally be handled by vetted cryptographic
libraries, key management systems, or platform services that understand
the requirements of the protocol.

5
00:00:22.976 --> 00:00:24.626
Salts are different from keys.

6
00:00:24.626 --> 00:00:32.243
In password hashing, a salt helps ensure that the same password does not
produce the same stored verifier across accounts when used correctly.

7
00:00:32.243 --> 00:00:38.272
Salts normally require uniqueness rather than secrecy, and they are
usually stored with the password verifier.

8
00:00:38.272 --> 00:00:45.000
A salt should not be treated as the main application secret, and a salt
does not replace password hashing cost or rate limiting.

9
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.681
Nonces and initialization values depend on protocol requirements.

10
00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.141
Some values must be unique.

11
00:00:50.141 --> 00:00:51.665
Some must be unpredictable.

12
00:00:51.665 --> 00:00:53.823
Some must never repeat with the same key.

13
00:00:53.823 --> 00:00:57.885
Misunderstanding this distinction can break cryptographic assumptions.

14
00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:01.693
Reuse can be dangerous in some contexts, even if the value is not secret.

15
00:01:01.693 --> 00:01:07.151
This is why routine application development should avoid assembling
cryptographic modes manually.

16
00:01:07.151 --> 00:01:12.927
Developers should rely on high-level cryptographic APIs that manage
randomness requirements correctly.

17
00:01:12.927 --> 00:01:22.257
If a library asks the application to supply a nonce, key, or
initialization value, developers should understand exactly what property
is required and how reuse is prevented.

18
00:01:22.257 --> 00:01:26.954
Custom cryptographic construction is not appropriate for ordinary
application work.

19
00:01:26.954 --> 00:01:32.920
The safer pattern is to choose libraries that reduce the number of
dangerous choices the application must make.
